WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.283
- All right.

00:00:01.283 --> 00:00:02.116
Well, it's no secret.

00:00:02.116 --> 00:00:03.960
It's been a rough couple
years for all of us.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.240
And mental health has been
at the top of the list.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.800
It has to be.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.830
But, help doesn't have to
look the same for everyone.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.560
One Brooklyn ray psychotherapist
says her clients,

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.720
who are primarily Latina women,

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.120
need more than just
traditional talk therapy.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.740
She's all about a hands-on approach

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.040
with breath-work, touch,
and ancestral practices.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.240
She's here to tell us about it.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.239
So, everybody give a warm
welcome to Christine.

00:00:26.239 --> 00:00:27.072
(audience clapping)

00:00:27.072 --> 00:00:27.905
Hi, Christine.

00:00:27.905 --> 00:00:28.738
- Thank you.

00:00:28.738 --> 00:00:29.924
- So-
- Thank you for having me.

00:00:29.924 --> 00:00:30.757
- No, you're than welcome.

00:00:30.757 --> 00:00:31.710
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.000
So, explain more about

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.580
how you've tailored your
approach for your clients.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.380
- Yeah, so when I first
became a therapist,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.780
I realized that the more
westernized approach

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.500
to therapy was a bit
more clinical and direct.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.170
And while that's super
important and necessary,

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.180
I also felt that with
our Latino community,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.310
we really crave that
warmth and that touch,

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.070
and I wanted to make it
approachable to our community.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.730
And so I incorporated
meditation and breath-work,

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.160
and we get to sit together in circle,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.440
and cry together and scream if we need to.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.070
And just really hold each
other as we break open.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.050
And it was through this work

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:12.050
that I decided to create
my book, "I Am Diosa."

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.310
And the Diosahood Community,

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.800
which is a space where women can come,

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.060
and learn about self esteem
and mental health resources.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.950
But, that really do so in a way

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.610
that connects to our culture and our root.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:27.623
- That's amazing.

00:01:27.623 --> 00:01:28.456
(audience clapping)

00:01:28.456 --> 00:01:31.470
It also sounds like Texas women
that I grew up with as well.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.610
Like, we love that as well.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.531
What you just described is
very relatable to me as well.

00:01:35.531 --> 00:01:36.364
- That's great.

00:01:36.364 --> 00:01:38.310
- So, you're going to
teach us all one exercise

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.143
to relieve stress.

00:01:39.143 --> 00:01:40.038
- So-

00:01:40.038 --> 00:01:40.950
